Outline of the Production System talk for the RTSC Workshop.
C. L. Forgy  17Nov78

[Comments about the talk are enclosed in square brackets.  In
particular, the slides to be used are so indicated.]


1. INTRODUCTION.

1.0. I am scheduled to discuss Production Systems (PSs) vis a vis
other AI languages.  The obvious place to start is to ask what are
the distinguishing characteristics of PSs.  Though others might
disagree, I would argue that there are two distinguishing
characteristics [SLIDE]
	a. Their heavy reliance on pattern directed evocation.
	b. Their abandoning of hierarchical control structures.
	   (Eg, The PS architecture includes no subroutine construct.)

1.1. Let me briefly describe the PSs we are currently using at CMU
[SLIDE showing a PS]
	a. A PS comprises two memories, Production Memory and Working
	   Memory.
	b. The contents of Production Memory are condition-action
	   pairs called Productions.
	c. The contents of Working Memory are simply symbol structures
	   of some kind.  In our present system they are lists.
	d. The condition part of each production is a collection
	   of patterns.
	e. The interpreter executes the PS by examining the condition
	   parts of all the productions, determining which match
	   elements in Working Memory (all the patterns in a condition
	   part must match elements), and then choosing one of those
	   condition parts and executing the corresponding action
	   part.
	f. The action part of the production can be thought of as
	   the body of a subroutine.  Since OPS2 is implemented in
	   Lisp, the action part is much like a Lisp PROG.
	g. The action part of the production will generally affect
	   the contents of Working Memory -- perhaps adding one or
	   more new lists and deleting one or more existing lists.
	   (The action parts are not required to change Working
	   Memory.)
	h. The action part can perform almost any operation.
	   One operation that is specifically disallowed is evoking
	   another production.  Only after the action part surrenders
	   control back to the interpreter is the next production
	   evoked.
	i. The interpreter chooses the next production to evoke in the
	   same way as before.  It examines all the condition parts,
	   and then selects from among those whose patterns are
	   satisfied.  If the production that just fired changed
	   Working Memory, the set of satisfied condition parts is
	   probably changed.
The heavy use of pattern directed evocation should be obvious now.
(In this context it might be noted that we generally try to make the
action parts of the productions short.)  The non-hierarchical nature
of the system derives from not allowing one production to call another
production.

1.3. People at CMU have been using PSs for a number of years now.  As
they gain more experience and  attempt more complex tasks, the PSs of
course become larger.  By their current standards, a system with 1000
productions would be considered quite large.  (To convert this number
into  more familiar terms,  consider it the equivalent  of 1000 short
Lisp functions -- or  perhaps more accurately 1000 Planner theorems.)
For philosophical reasons, an  attempt is made to keep Working Memory
small.  A typical Working Memory might average 100 or 200 lists, each
list containing about 10 atoms.

1.4. There is a community of researchers who believe that PSs are
appropriate for the building of intelligent programs.  Since this
is still an active research area, I choose not to argue either for
or against their position.  Instead, I am going to take one of the
PS's distinguishing characteristics and discuss its implications
for computer design.  This is a reasonable course for two reasons
	a. The characteristic I will focus on, the heavy reliance
	   on pattern directed evocation, is the feature that is of
	   greatest concern to implementors of PS interpreters.  As an
	   implementor myself, I can state that one tends to spend all
	   his time worrying about how to get the pattern matching 
	   done efficiently.  
	b. The algorithms and the hardware that have been developed
	   for this task would be useful for other programming
	   languages as well.  Since this is not at all obvious, I
	   will have more to say about it in a few minutes.


2. THE ALGORITHM.

2.1. [SLIDE showing an abstract network] For several years we at CMU
have been using an algorithm called the Rete Match Algorithm (RMA)
for our production systems.  The RMA is compiler based.  Before
execution of a program begins, the entire collection of patterns in
the program is compiled into a single graph structure.  This graph
consists of four parts.  The first part could be viewed as the dual
of a discrimination net; that is, it is a subgraph such that if one
converted the nodes into edges and the edges into nodes, a
discrimination net would result.  The objects in the data base are
filtered through this part of the graph so that each individual node
in the later parts of the graph sees only a few of the objects.  The
second part to the graph is a collection of nodes with memory.  A
memory node remembers every object that reaches it; consequently, on
successive activations of the graph, it is sufficient to feed through
the graph only the objects that have changed since the last
activation.  (If these nodes were not in the graph, it would be
necessary to process the entire data base.)  The third part of the
graph is a set of nodes which combine the objects passed by the
various paths in the first part of the graph.  This third part is
needed because some condition parts are conjunctions of several
patterns.  The last part of the graph is a connection back to the
rest of the PS interpreter.  The nodes in this part of the graph
inform the interpreter when a condition part is satisfied.

2.2. The RMA is not particularly restrictive in the kind of patterns
it will support.  For example in our current production system
(which uses list structured data objects) patterns may contain
[SLIDE]
	a. Constants, variables, and user-defined predicates.
	b. A limited form of segment variable.
	c. Pattern-and.
	d. Conjunction of patterns.
	e. Negation of patterns and conjunctions of patterns.
It could be extended to other things.  For example
	f. Data types other than lists.
	g. Pattern-or.

2.3. The RMA is efficient because
[SLIDE]
	a.  The compiler recognizes and exploits the similarities
	    in the patterns.
	b.  Since the memory nodes store information, the effort
	    does not depend strongly on the absolute size of the
	    data base.


3. THE TIME AND SPACE COSTS OF INTERPRETING A PS.

3.1. Before we can discuss the effort of interpreting a PS, it is
necessary to choose a measure of effort.  Counting the number of
primary memory references has been a fairly accurate measure for all
the cases we have looked at.  In all the implementations we have
considered, the amount of processing that takes place after each
memory reference is small, and even a fairly primitive processor
should be able to run at close to memory speed.

3.2. The amount of effort required to monitor changes in the data
base depends on
	a. The rate of change of the data base.
	b. The number of condition parts in the system.
	c. The size of the data base.
The dependence for the RMA is as follows
	a. The effort varies directly with the rate of change of the
	   data base.
	b. The effort of monitoring should be a log function of the
	   number of condition parts.  (Making some very reasonable
	   assumptions one can show that this is the best that any
	   algorithm can manage.)
	c. The effect of data base size depends on the complexity of
	   the condition parts.  If the user writes combinatorially 
	   difficult patterns, the effort will be high.  But if the 
	   allows it to, the RMA will do well here.


3.3. The effort of course also depends on the PS being interpreted.
Some PSs have more complex condition parts than others, and some have
longer action parts.  Let us ignore that for a moment and concentrate
on one typical PS.  The PH-632 PS, which solves textbook physics
problems, is a good example; it is neither the simplest nor the most
complex PS being used now.

3.4. Using PH-632 as the archetype, one can derive the following
cost formula.  If the PS contains P productions, and performs
W changes to Working Memory each second, the number of memory
references each second is

	16 * W * Log2(P)

From this, we can plot a family of curves showing the number of
memory references required to attain a given performance level.
[SLIDE]

3.5. To help make some sense from the graph
	a. A PS that performed about 100 Working Memory changes
	   each second would about equal a Lisp program run on
	   a KA-10. [cf. McCracken's thesis; Rychener's thesis]
	b. A KA-10 has a memory bandwidth of about 0.5 million
	   words per second.
	c. The Lisp Machine has a memory bandwidth of about
	   ? million words per second.

3.6. The space cost of the algorithm is linear.  The compiled form of
the typical PH-632 condition part requires about 20 words.  The
uncompiled condition part is about twice as big.


4. THE HARDWARE SUPPORT FOR THE ALGORITHM.

4.1. At one time we were considering use of a special function box to
perform the match. [SLIDE] But as our understanding of the task
increased, we discovered that nothing so extreme was needed.  We
found that by changing the format of the data and by giving more
responsibility to the compiler, we could get comparable performance
with very little hardware enhancement.

4.2. We now know how to support the Rete Match on an ordinary
von Neumann processor augmented with
[SLIDE]
	a. A few hundred words of writable control store.
	b. A few hundred words of high speed scratch pad memory.

4.3. Of course, if even that hardware is not available, the Rete
Match can be run on the processor.  We have made some estimates of
the benefit of the hardware.  The following table contains the
relative number of memory references that would be required by four
possible implementations.  It is normalized so that the
implementation just discussed shows 1.
[SLIDE]

	Implementation		Memory References

	Microcode and		  1
	Scratchpad

	Microcode alone		  3

	Assembly Language	 15

	Lisp (current OPS2)	150


5. PARALLELISM.

5.1. In order to achieve real time response from a large production
system, one would almost certainly have to use parallelism.  For
example, to execute a Hearsay-II PS in real time (a 1000 word
vocabulary) one would have to perform on the order of 40 million
memory references per second.

5.2. The Rete Match Algorithm is well suited to parallel 
implementation.  The match can operate with quite high degrees
of internal parallelism.  (The degree of parallelism possible
increases with the number of productions in the system.)

5.3. An interesting point about this kind of parallelism is that 
the user need not worry about it.  Since the parallelism is buried
in the interpreter, he sees and programs an ordinary, sequential
production system interpreter.


6. RELATIONSHIP OF PSs TO OTHER PROGRAMMING LANGUAGES.


6.1. The feature of PSs that we have been discussing might be called
pattern-directed monitoring of a changing data base.  I believe that
this control structure, or variations thereof, will become more and
more common in future AI programs.  Evidence for this can be found in
at least two quarters.
	a. The languages that are proposed for successors to Lisp
	   (Planner, QA4, Conniver, Qlisp, ...) generally include
	   mechanisms for pattern directed evocation of procedures.
	b. Programs that are written in Lisp or other languages 
	   that lack pattern matching often make use of specially
	   written pattern matchers.  Mycin is a well known
	   example of these programs.

6.2. Certainly the kinds of pattern-matching used by these systems
is not identical to that used in our PSs, but then the Rete Match
Algorithm is not specific to just those PSs.  It could, with the
use of an appropriate compiler, handle patterns like those in the
other languages as well.  

6.3. However, since the patterns used in most of the other languages
are rather restricted, one might want to change the patterns anyway.

6.4. If users of these languages do start writing more complex
patterns, or if they do start using pattern directed evocation
more frequently, then the efficiency of the pattern matcher in
the interpreter will become more important.  The algorithm and
the hardware described here will then also become more important.


7. CONCLUSIONS.

[SLIDE -- one for both]

7.1. The main conclusion is that with some quite inexpensive
hardware support, the match algorithm would provide efficient
pattern directed monitoring of changing data bases.

7.2. This is obviously of use to people who use PSs, but there is
reason to believe that it will also be of use to the users of other
programming languages.
